# Decisions

These pages record the load-bearing decisions behind tapedeck's first cut — the
ones that, if you pull on them later, change what the package *is* rather
than how a function is named.

tapedeck is record/replay middleware for the
[Vercel AI SDK](https://sdk.vercel.ai): wrap your model once, run your
agent test against the live API a single time, commit the cassette, and
every CI run after that is deterministic, offline, free, and
stream-accurate. Five choices make that work, and each one closed off an
obvious alternative. The pages below explain why.

They are short on purpose. If a v2 ticket starts arguing with something
cut here, the discussion belongs on its decision page, not on the v1
ticket.

## Pages

- [**Middleware, not a proxy**](/decisions/middleware-not-proxy) — why
  tapedeck lives at the `wrapLanguageModel` layer instead of an HTTP
  proxy (nock/Polly) or a hand-written `MockLanguageModelV3`. The
  flagship decision: normalize at the SDK abstraction, survive
  wire-format changes, replay streams as real streams, ship no infra.
- [**A miss throws in CI**](/decisions/miss-throws-in-ci) — why a
  `replay` cache miss raises `CassetteMissError` instead of silently
  falling back to the live API. A changed prompt or tool schema changes
  the hash, misses, and fails the build loudly — no stale fixtures.
- [**Redact at record time**](/decisions/redact-at-record-time) — why
  secret redaction happens when the cassette is written, not when it's
  read, so secrets never reach disk — and why a committed cassette that
  still carries a redactable value throws `CassetteSecretError`.
- [**Vercel AI SDK coupling**](/decisions/vercel-ai-sdk-coupling) — why
  tapedeck binds tightly to the AI SDK's language-model spec and is *not*
  a generic recorder, what `COMPATIBILITY.md` and the peer range buy us,
  and how the cassette `version` field surfaces drift.
- [**Compare never writes**](/decisions/compare-never-writes): why
  `compare` reports drift instead of refreshing the cassette, and why the
  tool-call trajectory is treated as the agent's contract while the prose
  around it is not.

## The shape of the bet

The first four are the same bet made four times: stand at
the SDK's own abstraction boundary and refuse to step below it. Below
that line is the provider wire format — where proxies live and break;
above it is your application code, which should not know whether a
response came from the network or from disk. tapedeck sits exactly on
the line, which is the only place all four guarantees —
provider-agnostic, stream-native, deterministic, secret-safe — hold at
once.

The fifth is the same instinct pointed at time rather than layers. A
fixture can be perfectly recorded and still stop describing the world,
because the model underneath it moved. `compare` says so out loud and
leaves the fixture alone, so the decision to accept the new behaviour
stays where the other four put it: in a diff, in a pull request, in front
of a person.
